# Anything not excluded here is copied into the image by `COPY backend/`, and
# an image layer is not a secret store: it can be pulled, exported and
# inspected by anyone with registry access, and deleting a file in a later
# layer does not remove it from the earlier one.
#
# EVERY PATTERN MEANT TO MATCH INSIDE A SUBDIRECTORY MUST BE `**/`-ANCHORED.
# This file is not .gitignore. Git re-applies a bare pattern at every
# directory level; Docker and Buildah match each pattern once against the
# whole path relative to the context root, so a bare `.env` excludes `./.env`
# and nothing else. The first version of this file used bare patterns and was
# asserted to work by a test that only checked the strings were present. The
# image built from it contained backend/.env (five live API keys), the 417MB
# host virtualenv and a 6.4MB SQLite cache. `backend/tests/` was the only rule
# that worked, because it names its path. Verified against real builds:
# `.env` leaves `sub/.env` in the image, `**/.env` removes it.

# Secrets. backend/.env holds every API key.
.env
.env.*
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example
**/*.pem
**/*.key

# Local virtualenvs — host-specific binaries that bloat the image and shadow
# the versions `pip install -r requirements.txt` just resolved.
**/.venv/
**/venv/
**/env/

# Caches and build output
**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]
**/.pytest_cache/
**/.mypy_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/
**/.coverage
**/htmlcov/

# Not needed at runtime
**/.git/
.github/
.gitignore
frontend/
# docs/ is at the context root, so a bare pattern is the correct form here —
# the `**/`-anchoring rule above is for patterns aimed inside a subdirectory.
# **/*.md already excluded the prose; this also keeps docs/reference/*.pdf
# (1.9MB of it) out of the context transfer.
docs/
**/*.pdf
**/node_modules/
**/.next/
**/*.md
!backend/**/*.md

# Tests do not run in the production image; they are run in CI against
# backend/requirements.txt.
backend/tests/

# Scratch / local data
**/*.log
**/.DS_Store

# The developer's SQLite response cache. Shipping it put 6.4MB of local query
# history in a published image layer, and because the image copies this tree as
# root and then runs as uid 10001, the baked-in database was readable but not
# writable — so a cache hit returned data and a cache miss raised
# OperationalError. The cache is runtime state; it is created at the writable
# path resolved in backend/data/data_cache.py, never built into the image.
**/*.db
**/*.sqlite
**/*.sqlite3
**/*.db-journal
**/*.db-wal
